INTRO = (
 "<p><strong>Why the review found these errors before we did.</strong> Each of the 25 findings was reproduced on version 9's code before anything was changed, and each one points at a gap in how version 9 was checked, not only at a line of code.</p>"
 "<ul>"
 "<li><strong>Tests written from the engine's own states.</strong> A test can only catch the violation of a state the model has. Version 9 had no pending payout, no locked account and no cancelled request, so no test could see trading during processing (findings 1, 20), a request made too early (2), or a request counted in the wrong session (18). We had read the firms' payout pages for numbers (days, splits, minimums); the review read them for the states they describe. Version 10's tests are written from the rule's text and from the review's own witnesses, and each correction has one (tests_v10b.py).</li>"
 "<li><strong>Rule lists built from the model.</strong> Version 9 listed the rules it modelled, so a rule it lacked was invisible: The5ers' payout cap and profit gate (16), Topstep's request session and minimum (18, 19), Alpha Capital's lot limit (4). Version 10's inventory starts from the firms' pages and lists every rule that can change what the plan may do or what it is paid, with how the model treats it; comparing it row by row with the engine's parameters then found FTMO's $20 minimum missing (n31).</li>"
 "<li><strong>Theorems used beyond what they prove.</strong> The martingale argument proves a trade's expected gross result; the text used it for values per month (24). The renewal argument needs a stationary distribution; the text applied it to a model whose price has none (11). The bridge bound holds for one bracket; the text applied it to a portfolio (9). Each proof was right for its statement, and we did not check that each use needed only that statement. Version 10 states every result with its scope and labels every estimate by what it measures.</li>"
 "<li><strong>Numbers computed by code that the text did not describe.</strong> Two comparisons averaged per-path ratios while the text compared ratios of totals (23); three validation intervals assumed independence across trades on four reused paths (22), in code written before the clustering correction and never revisited; a sensitivity changed a second input it did not name (25). Version 10 keeps per-path results for every statistic, computes every comparison between like estimators, and checks that an intervention changes only what it names (the filler variant is now identical to the baseline attempt by attempt when no filler is executed).</li>"
 "<li><strong>Prose written before the numbers.</strong> Sentences such as 'changes the result measurably' were written once and kept while the numbers changed. Version 10 generates every such reading from the interval it describes.</li>"
 "</ul>"
 "<p>We also read version 9's text claim by claim against the code and the data before writing version 10: every number recomputed, every 'every', 'never' and 'none' checked against what the code guarantees, and the rule facts against the firms' pages where we read them again (the inventory dates every row). That reading found the errors of the table below; together with the review's they fall into seven classes: labels that promised more than the estimator gives, printed arithmetic that did not reproduce, universal claims the code did not guarantee, rule facts carried over without re-reading, intervals that ignored the path clustering, interventions that changed more than they said, and template text wrong for some programmes.</p>")

